<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Seljuk Sultanate of Syria</h1><p class="st-meta">1079 CE – 1095 CE · capital Damascus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Syrian realm of Tutush, brother of the Seljuk sultan Malik-Shah, whose death split Syria just before the First Crusade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1079 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah</a> sent his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush</a> to aid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atsiz-ibn-uvaq">Atsiz</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>; Tutush had Atsiz killed and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. In 1086 he defeated and killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Rum Seljuk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and extended his rule over most of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush</a> ruled as a Seljuk prince with Turkish commanders as governors, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-sunqur-al-hajib">Aq Sunqur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/artuqids-of-mardin">Artuqids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The state rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a> land grants, and its revenues came from the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and Aleppo and the trade of the coast. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah</a> died in 1092, Tutush claimed the sultanate itself and executed Aq Sunqur in 1094.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush</a> was killed in battle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a> in 1095 fighting his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkyaruq">Berkyaruq</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> was divided between his sons: Ridwan in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seljuk-emirate-of-aleppo">Seljuk Emirate of Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seljuk-emirate-of-damascus">Seljuk Emirate of Damascus</a>, whose rivalry left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> divided when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> arrived.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1079 CE – 1095 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.17%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1079 CE – 1095 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Damascus</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tutush I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkic, Persian, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Syrian Seljuks, Seljuks of Syria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1087&amp;focus=seljuk-sultanate-of-syria" aria-label="Seljuk Sultanate of Syria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox